Deploy kit evidence (Lane R4-C)
Date: 2026-09-05
Repo: ~/Claude/sotto, main (d9b63a0 at lane start; HEAD == origin/main confirmed)
Docker: 29.6.1 (Docker Desktop 4.81.0), docker-credential-desktop symlinked
per KICKOFF-4/CLAUDE.md.

=== 1. Build ===
$ docker compose build
(first build, cold cache): 8m01s wall clock, image 10.2GB.
Root cause of the bloat: apps/client/ios (the native Xcode project + Pods,
~4.1GB) was being sent into the build context — this repo never runs
`expo run:ios` inside the container, only `expo export --platform web`.
Added `apps/client/ios` (and `apps/client/android`, not present but future-
proofed) to .dockerignore.

$ docker compose build --no-cache   (after the .dockerignore fix)
4m38s wall clock, image 4.49GB.
Remaining size is mostly the full pnpm install (1.7GB node_modules,
including devDependencies — required, since apps/server has no compile
step and runs its TypeScript source directly via tsx at runtime, a
devDependency; see Dockerfile's top comment) plus packages/content/packs
(132MB of narrated-book content, shipped twice: once for the client's
static export at apps/client/dist/content/packs, once for apps/server's
own /content/packs/* route which reads packages/content/packs directly —
apps/server/src/app.ts's CONTENT_PACKS_DIR is a fixed path three
directories above its own source, so the runtime image keeps the
monorepo's relative layout intact rather than a single flattened bundle).
Not slimmed further in this lane (pnpm deploy / a prod-only stage) — see
"Escalate" note at the bottom for the one change that would need it.

=== 2. Cold start ===
$ docker compose up -d
Time from `up -d` returning to the first HTTP 200 on `/`: 4.6s
(measured via a polling loop against the published port; server log
confirms VAD warm-up + `Server listening` complete before the first
successful `/health` request).

Idle memory once warmed (`docker stats --no-stream`): ~195MB RSS. This is
the basis for fly.toml.example's 512MB VM note (comfortably under it at
idle; the note flags the tighter case with the VAD model loaded + WS
sessions live, not measured here since no live tutor turns ran under
`docker stats` sampling).

NOTE on port 8790: this Mac had an unrelated local `tsx` dev-server process
already bound to host port 8790 (not part of this lane, not started by it —
looked like a leftover from the concurrent content session's own local
dev loop) which silently intercepted `curl 127.0.0.1:8790/*` even while
`docker compose up` also had 8790 published (dual-stack IPv4/IPv6 bind
quirk on macOS — the container held the IPv6 wildcard, the stray host
process held IPv4). This produced misleading 404s on `/` and `/index.html`
even though the container was healthy and correctly serving `/health` and
`/content/packs` on its own. Diagnosed via `lsof -i :8790` showing two
listeners. Not a Dockerfile/compose bug — verified by remapping the host
port (8792, briefly, then reverted) and getting clean 200s with matching
Fastify request logs at every step. Shipped docker-compose.yml keeps the
sane default (8790:8790); if you hit the same symptom on your own machine,
check `lsof -i :8790` for a stray process before suspecting the image.

